Home Lab #1: Setting Up a Windows 11 VM in VirtualBox (and Fixing What Went Wrong)

1 November 2026

Goal
I built a Windows 11 virtual machine in VirtualBox so I can practise help desk troubleshooting and bring my Windows support skills up to date. It means I can experiment and break things without any risk to my gaming laptop. If I turn the VM into an unbootable mess, I just restore a snapshot and I'm back where I started. No reinstall, no lost games, no stress.

Environment
Host: gaming laptop with 16 GB RAM and 6 cores
Hypervisor: VirtualBox 7
Guest: Windows 11 Pro with 8 GB RAM, 3 CPUs and a 64 GB dynamically allocated disk
EFI, TPM 2.0 and Secure Boot enabled

Issue 1: VM failed to boot

Symptom: VirtualBox said the virtual machine failed to boot, possibly because of a missing operating system or wrong boot order.

Cause: I created the VM before the Windows 11 ISO had finished downloading, so there was nothing attached to boot from.

Fix: I deleted the VM, waited for the download to finish, then rebuilt it with the ISO attached.

Issue 2: VM went to the UEFI menu

Symptom: Instead of starting the Windows installer, the VM opened the UEFI firmware menu.

Cause: The installer waits a few seconds for you to press a key to boot from CD or DVD. I didn't press one in time, so the VM tried the empty hard disk, found nothing, and fell back to the UEFI menu.

Fix: I went into Boot Manager, picked the CD-ROM drive and pressed a key at the prompt. The installer started.

Note: The automatic install didn't kick in, so Windows asked for a product key. I finished the setup manually with no product key, Windows 11 Pro, and a custom install onto the new virtual disk.

Snapshots

I took a snapshot of the Windows 11 VM called CleanInstall. It's my safe starting point, so I can break things during labs and roll back in seconds.

I also took a snapshot of my Kali Linux VM called CleanKali from the VirtualBox Manager while it was powered off.

Lessons learned

Make sure the ISO has fully downloaded before creating the VM.

Know your way around the UEFI Boot Manager when a VM won't boot.

Be ready to press a key at the boot prompt.

Skills shown

Virtualisation, Windows 11 installation, boot troubleshooting, UEFI, snapshots and recovery

